  {# --- where data lives ----------------------------------------------------
     The section this page mostly exists for. "The database" sounds like one thing and is four,
     and three of the four are not the one people mean. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="data-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="data-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-2">Where data lives</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Four places, and they hold very different things. The weather, the departures and the
      Formula 1 data are not stored anywhere.
    </p>

    <ul class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Firebase Authentication</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          Email addresses and password hashes. The browser trades a password for a signed token, and
          the API only ever sees the token. Deleting an account here is what actually stops somebody
          signing in; everything else is a record about them.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Firestore</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          One document per account: name, role, approval state, home location, home stop, favourite
          teams. The role
          is read from here on <em>every single request</em>. It is a separate store from the one
          above, which is why an account can exist with no document and why erasure removes both.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Postgres</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          Seventeen tables. Contact submissions, a row per process start, which migrations have run, and
          the one-row table holding the secret behind the visitor number below. Four hold the chat,
          for signed-in users only: conversations, messages, notes and the Memory switch. One holds a
          trace of every chat question (30 days), described under the chat below. Seven the
          system writes for itself: the status page's health checks (seven days), the shape of each
          upstream's answers and what changed in it (field names and types, never a value), GitHub
          webhook deliveries that passed their signature check (30 days), the bus timetable, the
          sports data, saved so a restart does not fetch them again, and a nightly copy of the accounts,
          so that the backup covers them. And
          one row per page view: path, which of the two properties, the referring host, the time,
          a coarse browser, OS, device class and width bracket, the country as two letters (looked up
          on the server from the address; IP geolocation by
          <a href="https://db-ip.com" class="underline">DB-IP</a>, CC BY 4.0), and a visitor number that is a
          64-bit hash of a 24-hour secret, the address, the User-Agent and the property. The server
          feeds the address and the User-Agent into that hash and stores neither. It cuts the
          referring URL down to its host, sets no cookie, and erases the number from the row after
          30 days. The secret is replaced every 24 hours and the old one is destroyed rather than
          kept, which is what stops one day's numbers being matched to the next day's. The data view
          reads an allowlist of tables. That secret, the chat tables and the traces are not on it.
          Browsable on
          <a href="/admin/data/" class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">the data view</a>
          if you are an admin. A contact submission can be deleted one row at a time from
          <a href="/admin/messages/" class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">Messages</a>,
          or all of one person's at once by erasure.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Memory</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          Every upstream answer is cached in the API process for between 15 seconds and a day,
          and lost on restart, except the sports data and the bus timetable, which are also saved to Postgres. A bus or a
          race lives in memory and nowhere else, so there is no history of either to browse.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    {# Added the day erasure was built, because until then this section described four stores
       nothing could take anything out of, and a page called "where data lives" that cannot say
       how data leaves is only half a map. The last two sentences are the ones that matter: the
       emailed copy is real, it is outside all four stores, and no endpoint here reaches it. #}
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mt-4 max-w-prose">
      Until recently nothing in this system could take a person out of any of them. It can now:
      erasure takes one email address and removes that address's contact messages and its
      account's chat rows from Postgres, its account document from Firestore and its sign-in from Firebase Authentication, then
      answers with a receipt of what it actually removed instead of a success message. One
      action across three stores, because that is how far a person is spread: deleting from one
      leaves the other two still holding them.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mt-4 max-w-prose">
      There is a fifth place, and it is the honest part of this page. A message sent through the
      contact form is stored <em>and</em> emailed, so a copy sits in a mailbox and in the mail
      provider's delivery log. Neither is a store this code owns, and nothing in the endpoint list
      below reaches into them. The erasure preview and the receipt both say so, in the API's own
      words, so nothing reports a deletion wider than the one that happened.
    </p>

    {# The two properties count differently, and a reader who noticed the banner on one site and
       not on the other deserves the reason rather than the appearance of an oversight. #}
    <h3 class="mt-8 font-semibold text-brand-text">Why the two sites count differently</h3>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mt-2 max-w-prose">
      The public site counts only after you say yes. The banner is opt-in, declining means nothing
      is counted at all, and that has not changed. What changed is only where the count goes: to the
      API above, not to a third party.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mt-4 max-w-prose">
      This app counts every page load, with no banner. It is behind a login, everyone here is an
      approved account holder, and counting loads of your own service for the running of that
      service is operational telemetry, not audience measurement. A row here carries the same
      visitor number the public site's rows carry, and on the same terms. The secret behind it is
      replaced every 24 hours, and the number is erased from the row after 30 days.
      The legal basis is different, so the consent is too. The line that keeps that honest is being told, which is what this
      paragraph and <a href="https://tzortzoglou.eu/privacy/" rel="noopener"
        class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">the privacy notice</a> are for; a
      count nobody is told about would be the same count with the honesty taken out.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mt-4 max-w-prose">
      "Everyone here is a known account holder" has to be true of the code, not only of the intent,
      and for a while it was not: these pages are static files that anyone can fetch, and the
      sign-in is a redirect JavaScript performs once one has loaded, so a stranger opening
      <code>/transit/</code> was counted on the way to being bounced. The beacon now fires from
      <code>shell.js</code> only after <code>/me</code> has confirmed the session, which is the
      earliest moment that sentence means anything. Not signed in, not counted.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mt-4 max-w-prose">
      Erasure does not reach this table, and an email address is not a handle it could be pointed
      at. A view row carries no account and no address. It does carry the number, which groups one
      browser's page loads inside a single day and stops there, because the next day's secret is a
      new one and the old one is gone. After 30 days the server erases the number and leaves the
      row as a path, a time and four coarse browser facts.
    </p>
  </section>

  {# --- the chat -------------------------------------------------------------
     Describes the design, and leaves out every number an abuser could plan around: the limits, the
     queue length and the cap live in the Owner's notes. So does where the model runs. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="chat-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="chat-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-2">The chat</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      The panel behind the corner button answers questions about this site, the app and how they are
      built. It runs an open model, Gemma 4 through llama.cpp, on a second server of mine. Questions go
      to no AI company. Only the API talks to the model, and the model has no tools and sees only what
      the API hands it. It is not one of the twenty-five upstreams, because it is mine, or one of the
      three deployables, because nothing here builds it: it is a pinned image, started by hand.
    </p>

    <ul class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Routing</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          Keywords in the question pick the matching sections of the fact sheet, and earlier questions
          break ties, so a follow-up lands in the right place. If nothing matches, the chat says it
          does not know and the model is never called. The same goes for a question that names a
          technology the fact sheet does not have, whatever else in it matches.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">The fact sheet</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          The model reads short lines written by hand, not the whole site. Every line carries a quote
          that must appear in the public page it cites, and a check over the built pages fails if a
          quote has gone, as does one over the live pages when the API deploys. The sources under an answer come from the routed sections, never from the
          model's text.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Finding the facts</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          Each technical line of the fact sheet is held in the API as an embedding, a list of numbers
          for its meaning. A second small model on the same server does the same to the question, and
          the closest lines go to the chat model beside the keyword hits, in place of whole sections.
          A question the keywords miss gets this second try, unless it is about a person. Lines about
          me are not in that index. If the second model is down, keywords carry on alone.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Live tools</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          The weather, the next race, the standings, departures, results and site status come from
          read-only tools. Code picks the tool from keywords and code writes the sentences from its
          result, so the model is not involved. Departures need a sign-in, and the account comes from
          the sign-in check, never from the question.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Guardrails</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          A question that tries to steer the model gets the fixed reply before routing, and an earlier
          question, a note or a stored turn that does the same is dropped. Email addresses, phone
          numbers, card-like numbers and long digit runs are masked in the model's sentences before
          they leave.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">The cache</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          A plain question the model answered is held in the API's memory for a short time, so the
          same question costs no model time. An answer that used a tool, notes or earlier questions
          never is, and a change to the facts or the prompt leaves the old answers unreachable.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Traces</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          Questions are kept for 30 days, masked, with no IP address or account. Each one leaves a row
          with the route, the lines picked and their scores, the tool, how it ended and the timings.
          It holds no answer, and with Memory off no question text either. An admin page reads them,
          and the data view cannot. A row isn't linked to an account, so erasure doesn't reach it, and they go by the 30-day limit.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">The eval gate</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          A set of test questions runs against the real model by hand, because CI cannot reach it. A
          full pass records a hash of the prompt, facts, retrieval, checks, tools and questions, and
          the API's test suite fails if any of them changes without a new pass.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">The sentence check</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          The API holds each sentence until it ends, checks it, and only then sends it. A sentence
          fails if it has a technology, number, employer or qualification the check can tell is not
          in the fact sheet, opens with yes or no, has no word from the fact sheet, or names me, because
          the model only ever gets the technical sections. If the question may be about me, every
          sentence counts as a claim about me: each word has to come from the fact sheet, and a
          negative or a bare "it" fails unless the sentence is word for word in the fact sheet. It is
          a word check, so it can miss one. The check is code, not a second model. A failure stops
          the answer with a fixed line saying it isn't stated on the site. Answers are shown as plain
          text, never as HTML.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Answers about me</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          Lines about me never reach the model. A question that lands only on a section about me
          never does either: code returns the matching lines of the fact sheet word for word, so
          nothing can be invented. A question that also touches the site gets the model's sentences
          about the site, then those lines, added by code.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">The queue</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          Questions take turns on the model. Others wait in a short queue and the panel shows
          their place; past that comes a plain busy reply. Closing the panel cancels the answer and
          frees the model. If the model is down the panel says it is resting and the rest of the
          system carries on. The logs hold no question or answer text, for anyone.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Memory</h3>
        <p class="hint mt-2">
          For signed-in users only, and every query a user can reach filters on the signed-in account. Conversations are
          stored as they happened. Once one goes quiet, a background job turns the user's questions into
          short notes, when nobody is waiting for the model, and code throws away any note that
          mentions me, reads like an instruction or touches a sensitive subject. A few relevant notes
          go to the model in their own block, labelled as things the user said. They shape emphasis and
          length, they never feed routing, and the sentence check ignores them, so a note cannot
          support a claim. <a href="/remember/" class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">What I
          remember</a> shows all of it, with edit, delete and a Memory switch. Rows go after 12 months
          without activity, or sooner by erasure, and
          <a href="https://tzortzoglou.eu/privacy/#chat-memory" rel="noopener"
            class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">the privacy notice</a> has the rest.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  {# --- endpoints ----------------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="api-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="api-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-2">The endpoints</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Every route the API serves, and the lowest role that may call it, grouped and folded away
      on a narrow screen. Each one re-reads your role
      and approval from Firestore before doing anything, because a token proves who signed in, not
      what they may do now.
    </p>

    {# Fetched rather than built into this page, and this is the reason:
       app.tzortzoglou.eu is a directory of static files. Its sign-in is a redirect in
       JavaScript, which decides what is drawn and guards nothing at all - every page here is
       served in full to anyone who asks for the URL. A complete list of endpoints and the role
       each one needs is the most useful thing on this page to somebody who has no account, so
       it is the one part that is not in the HTML. It comes from the API, which asks who you are.

       It also stopped being a hand-kept copy in the process. The list now lives beside the
       router in the API repository, and that repository's test suite fails if the two disagree
       in either direction. #}
    <div id="endpoints" class="mt-4"></div>
    <p id="endpointsNote" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="hint mt-4">Loading the endpoint list…</p>

  </section>

  {# --- upstreams ------------------------------------------------------------
     The section that replaced an entity-relationship diagram of three tables nobody stores
     anything interesting in. "We call an API" describes nothing; which endpoint, how often it
     may be called, and whose field became which of ours is where the work actually is. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="up-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="up-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-2">The upstreams, in detail</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mb-6 max-w-prose">
      Twenty-four of the twenty-five are called by the server. Six of those need a credential, which has to
      stay on the server; the rest so that one call serves every reader, and for the three sports
      sources, so a page reads a saved copy rather than three unofficial sites. The twenty-fifth is the
      basemap, and it is not proxied at all: your browser fetches its tiles itself, which makes it
      the only one your browser has ever heard of, along with the satellite imagery on the two maps that offer it. ESPN, shl.se and hockeyallsvenskan.se share one
      entry below, because they do one job, and so do the two papers. Each one below lists what is called, how long an answer
      is held, and how their field names become ours.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mb-6 max-w-prose">
      In the mapping tables, theirs is on the left and ours on the right. A row marked
      <em>derived</em> is something nobody sent: it is worked out here, and those rows are the
      decisions, not the transport.
    </p>

    {%- for up in upstreams %}
    <details class="group card mb-4" {% if loop.first %}open{% endif %}>
      <summary class="flex cursor-pointer select-none flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2
        list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden focus-visible:outline-none
        focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-brand-accent rounded">
        <span class="text-base font-bold text-brand-text">{{ up.name }}</span>
        <span class="doc-role">{% if up.key %}key{% else %}no key{% endif %}</span>
        <span class="flex-1 text-xs text-brand-muted">held {{ up.cache }}</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-brand-muted transition-transform duration-150 group-open:rotate-180" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
      </summary>

      <p class="hint mt-2 max-w-prose">{{ up.what }}</p>

      <h3 class="mt-6 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-muted">What is called</h3>
      <div class="mt-2">
        {%- for call in up.calls %}
        <div class="doc-row">
          <code class="text-xs text-brand-text">{{ call.path }}</code>
          <span class="text-sm text-brand-muted">{{ call.note }}</span>
        </div>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-6 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-muted">
        How it is mapped
      </h3>
      <div class="mt-2">
        {%- for row in up.mapping %}
        <div class="map-row">
          <span class="map-from">{{ row[0] }}</span>
          <span class="shrink-0 text-brand-muted" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span class="map-to">
            {{ row[1] }}{% if row[2] %} <span class="text-[10px] font-sans uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-accent">derived</span>{% endif %}
          </span>
        </div>
        {#- An upstream that answers with images has no field names to rename, and a table with no
            rows in it under a heading that promises one is worse than a sentence. #}
        {%- else %}
        <p class="hint max-w-prose">{{ up.mappingNote }}</p>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-6 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-brand-muted">Worth knowing</h3>
      <p class="hint mt-2 max-w-prose">{{ up.why }}</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
        {%- for note in up.notes %}
        <li class="text-base text-brand-muted max-w-prose">{{ note }}</li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </details>
    {%- endfor %}

    <p class="hint mt-4 max-w-prose">
      The two credentials are read from the environment on the server and stay out of every
      response, error message and log line. Upstream errors are reported by status code only,
      because an error body can echo the request back, and the request carries the key.
    </p>
  </section>

  {# --- conventions ---------------------------------------------------------- #}
  <section aria-labelledby="rules-heading" class="mt-10">
    <h2 id="rules-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-2">Rules the code follows</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
      Each of these is here because breaking it caused a real bug in this codebase.
    </p>
    <ul class="card space-y-4 text-base text-brand-text">
      <li>
        <strong>Check the type before coercing a number.</strong> <code>Number(null)</code> is
        <code>0</code>, <code>Number([])</code> is <code>0</code>, and a query string can produce an
        array without being asked. Every parser guards on <code>typeof</code> first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An unknown role is the lowest role.</strong> Role names are an explicit list, not
        case-folding. Anything unrecognised resolves to the most restrictive answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A comment may not claim a control the code does not have.</strong> If the code
        cannot be made true, the comment changes in the same commit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A signal that was never about the thing being checked proves nothing about it.</strong>
        A green test suite that never loads a module says nothing about that module.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never invent data on a dashboard.</strong> A panel with no source behind it says so.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</section>
